ux-manual#5-freeze-qqs-hot-update","content":"5\\. Freeze QQ's hot-update","locale":"en"},"2300":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#6-download-and-run-snowluma-lite","content":"6\\. Download and run SnowLuma (lite)","locale":"en"},"2301":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"7\\. Launch QQ, scan, open the WebUI","locale":"en"},"2302":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#environment-dependent-caveats-be-candid","content":"Environment-dependent caveats (be candid)","locale":"en"},"2303":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"8\\. Keeping the session alive","locale":"en"},"2304":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"See also","locale":"en"},"2305":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","content":"On Linux, **Docker is the recommended and the only officially supported path**: Docker deployment bundles Linux QQ + Xvfb + VNC + noVNC + supervisord + ptrace permissions + hot-update freeze, ready to go. This page **replicates by hand what the image does** — for advanced users who **can't or won't use Docker**. It has many steps and many footguns, and it depends heavily on your distro and kernel settings. If you just want it running, go back to Docker deployment.","locale":"en"},"2306":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","content":"This page is the manual version of the official image's `start.sh`: install Node, install Linux QQ, bring up a headless X desktop, grant ptrace to node, freeze QQ's hot-update, run SnowLuma, scan-login. The commands below target &#x2A;*Ubuntu 24.04 (Noble)**; package names and paths differ on other distros — translate accordingly.","locale":"en"},"2307":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Manual deployment is the same things the image does, in order:","locale":"en"},"2308":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Install Node 24 LTS (the lite distribution ships no Node runtime).","locale":"en"},"2309":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Install Linux QQ (official `.deb`) plus its Electron / CJK dependencies.","locale":"en"},"2310":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Bring up a headless X desktop (Xvfb + fluxbox) + VNC / noVNC so you can scan the QR.","locale":"en"},"2311":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Grant `cap_sys_ptrace` to node **without root** (the hook injects via ptrace).","locale":"en"},"2312":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Freeze QQ's silent hot-update (black-hole its patch host) to protect the version-pinned native hook.","locale":"en"},"2313":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Download and unpack the SnowLuma `-lite` linux tarball, run `node ./index.mjs`.","locale":"en"},"2314":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#overview","content":"Launch QQ with the documented flags, scan-login via noVNC, open the WebUI.","locale":"en"},"2315":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#1-install-node-24-lts","content":"SnowLuma needs **Node.js ≥ 22**. In 2026, **Node 24 (\"Krypton\") is Active LTS** and 22 (\"Jod\") is in Maintenance LTS — for a fresh install go with 24. The lite distribution has no bundled runtime, so this step is mandatory.","locale":"en"},"2316":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#1-install-node-24-lts","content":"Pick one on Debian/Ubuntu:","locale":"en"},"2317":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#1-install-node-24-lts","content":"Node releases: Node release schedule. With nvm, `setcap` must hit the **real node binary nvm resolved**; redo it after every `nvm install` / `nvm use`.","locale":"en"},"2318":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#1-install-node-24-lts","content":"If you downloaded the **full** tarball (which bundles a Node runtime), you can skip this step — but the full package is much larger. This page follows the more common **lite** flow throughout.","locale":"en"},"2319":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#2-install-linux-qq-and-dependencies","content":"The official Linux QQ download page is JS-rendered, so **don't hard-code a version** — grab the current `.deb` for your arch from https\\://im.qq.com/linuxqq/download.html:","locale":"en"},"2320":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#2-install-linux-qq-and-dependencies","content":"QQ installs to `/opt/QQ/`, with the launcher at `/opt/QQ/qq`.","locale":"en"},"2321":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#2-install-linux-qq-and-dependencies","content":"Install the Electron / CJK runtime deps. &#x2A;*On Ubuntu 24.04 it's `libasound2t64`, not `libasound2`**:","locale":"en"},"2322":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#2-install-linux-qq-and-dependencies","content":"Without `fonts-noto-cjk`, Chinese text in QQ renders as tofu boxes.","locale":"en"},"2323":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Login is **QR-scan only** — there is no CLI login, so you must be able to *see* the QQ window to scan it. On a headless server, Xvfb provides a virtual screen, fluxbox lets QQ's login dialog map properly, and x11vnc + noVNC project that screen into a browser.","locale":"en"},"2324":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Install (Ubuntu 24.04 package names):","locale":"en"},"2325":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Start the virtual screen and set `DISPLAY` (the image uses `:1`):","locale":"en"},"2326":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Start a window manager (otherwise the login dialog may be borderless / unfocusable):","locale":"en"},"2327":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Start VNC (**set a password** — it lands in `~/.vnc/passwd`):","locale":"en"},"2328":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Start noVNC (browser-based VNC, no client needed). On Ubuntu the launcher is at `/usr/share/novnc/utils/novnc_proxy`:","locale":"en"},"2329":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"Then open `http://<your-ip>:6081/vnc.html` in a browser, connect with the VNC password you just set, and you'll see the `:1` virtual desktop.","locale":"en"},"2330":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#3-headless-x-desktop--vnc--novnc","content":"**Never expose VNC (5900) / noVNC (6081) / WebUI (5099) / OneBot (3000, 3001) raw on the public internet.** Bind them to localhost behind a firewall, an SSH tunnel, or an authenticated reverse proxy. Always set a strong VNC password — otherwise your QQ desktop is wide open.","locale":"en"},"2331":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"SnowLuma's native addon uses `ptrace` to inject the hook into the QQ process. Rather than run as root, give the node binary a file capability:","locale":"en"},"2332":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-39","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"Verify:","locale":"en"},"2333":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-40","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"setcap caveats (read these):","locale":"en"},"2334":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-41","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"**Must be applied to the real binary** — `readlink -f` resolves the symlink behind `which node`; the capability does not follow symlinks.","locale":"en"},"2335":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-42","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"**Dropped on node upgrade / copy** — re-run `setcap` after every Node upgrade.","locale":"en"},"2336":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-43","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"**Needs an xattr-capable filesystem** — `setcap` fails on some overlay / tmpfs mounts.","locale":"en"},"2337":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-44","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#4-grant-ptrace-to-node-without-root","content":"Subject to the kernel's &#x2A;*Yama `ptrace_scope`**: `0` / `1` / `2` all work with the file capability, but `3` disables ptrace entirely. Check with `cat /proc/sys/kernel/yama/ptrace_scope`.","locale":"en"},"2338":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-45","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#5-freeze-qqs-hot-update","content":"QQ has a **silent hot-update** channel that quietly rewrites bytes in the background and breaks your version-pinned native hook. (The \"Update now\" full-package dialog in the UI is click-gated and never fires headless, so you only need to block the silent channel.) Black-hole the patch host:","locale":"en"},"2339":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-46","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#5-freeze-qqs-hot-update","content":"Now QQ can't fetch silent patches, the version stays stable, and the hook won't be swapped out from under you.","locale":"en"},"2340":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-47","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#6-download-and-run-snowluma-lite","content":"Download the &#x2A;*`-lite`** linux tarball for your arch from the GitHub Releases: https\\://github.com/SnowLuma/SnowLuma/releases","locale":"en"},"2341":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-48","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#6-download-and-run-snowluma-lite","content":"On first run it creates its config in the data dir (`config/onebot.json`, `config/runtime.json`, etc.) and **logs a one-time WebUI admin password** — note it down, it appears only on a brand-new data dir's first boot.","locale":"en"},"2342":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-49","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#6-download-and-run-snowluma-lite","content":"Default services: OneBot HTTP `0.0.0.0:3000`, OneBot WS `0.0.0.0:3001`, WebUI `0.0.0.0:5099`. See Configuration for details. Environment variables override `runtime.json`.","locale":"en"},"2343":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-50","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Keep SnowLuma running (foreground or another session), then launch QQ under the &#x2A;*same `DISPLAY=:1`** with the documented flags:","locale":"en"},"2344":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-51","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Then:","locale":"en"},"2345":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-52","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Open `http://<your-ip>:6081/vnc.html`, connect over VNC, and you'll see QQ's QR code.","locale":"en"},"2346":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-53","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Scan it with QQ on your phone to log in.","locale":"en"},"2347":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-54","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Once login completes, the hook auto-promotes from passive observation to working mode (`runtime.json.hookAutoLoad` defaults on; set `false` or `SNOWLUMA_HOOK_AUTOLOAD=0` to revert to manual Load in the WebUI).","locale":"en"},"2348":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-55","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Open `http://<your-ip>:5099/` for the WebUI and log in with the bootstrap password from the logs.","locale":"en"},"2349":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-56","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#7-launch-qq-scan-open-the-webui","content":"Multiple accounts need no extra config: SnowLuma walks every QQ main process, injects each, and spins up one OneBot instance per UIN with its own `config/onebot_<uin>.json`. The key to running more than one is giving each QQ instance its &#x2A;*own `HOME`** (QQ ships a single-instance lock).","locale":"en"},"2350":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-57","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#environment-dependent-caveats-be-candid","content":"The manual flow is sensitive to your environment. These bits depend heavily on your machine:","locale":"en"},"2351":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-58","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#environment-dependent-caveats-be-candid","content":"**Package names / paths** vary by distro (above assumes Ubuntu 24.04). Debian, Arch, and RHEL-family systems differ on `libasound`, the X tools, and the noVNC launcher path.","locale":"en"},"2352":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-59","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#environment-dependent-caveats-be-candid","content":"**setcap** fails on some containerized / overlay / tmpfs filesystems (see step 4).","locale":"en"},"2353":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-60","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#environment-dependent-caveats-be-candid","content":"**Yama `ptrace_scope=3`** disables ptrace injection entirely.","locale":"en"},"2354":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-61","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#environment-dependent-caveats-be-candid","content":"If any step gets stuck in your environment, **the easiest path remains Docker deployment** — the image has already smoothed over all of this.","locale":"en"},"2355":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-62","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"When SSH drops, every process you started with `&` (Xvfb / VNC / QQ / SnowLuma) gets `SIGHUP` and dies. Login state lives in QQ's `HOME`; **if the processes are gone, the QR window is gone too**. Long-running hosts need a supervisor.","locale":"en"},"2356":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-63","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"Get the flow working in **tmux** first (QR, WebUI, send a message). Then move to **systemd**. The Docker path does not have this problem: `--restart unless-stopped` already supervises the stack.","locale":"en"},"2357":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-64","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"All of these processes must share `DISPLAY=:1`, in order: Xvfb, window manager, VNC / noVNC, QQ, SnowLuma.","locale":"en"},"2358":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-65","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"`loginctl enable-linger` keeps the user systemd instance after SSH logout. Logs: `journalctl --user -u snowluma-stack -f`. After editing the script: `systemctl --user restart snowluma-stack`.","locale":"en"},"2359":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-66","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"The official host-mode installer splits Xvfb / fluxbox / VNC / noVNC / QQ / SnowLuma into units under `snowluma.target`. Hand-rolling can start with this one wrapper; split later using `templates/host`.","locale":"en"},"2360":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-67","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"Do not treat `nohup … &` as a long-running solution: no restart policy, no unified logs.","locale":"en"},"2361":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-68","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"The systemd user unit's `HOME` must be the same one QQ used when you scanned the QR.","locale":"en"},"2362":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-69","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"The `novnc_proxy` path varies; Debian/Ubuntu often uses `/usr/share/novnc/utils/novnc_proxy`.","locale":"en"},"2363":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-70","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#8-keeping-the-session-alive","content":"After upgrading Node, run `setcap` again or injection fails on the next restart.","locale":"en"},"2364":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-71","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"Choosing a deployment — per-platform comparison","locale":"en"},"2365":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-72","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"Docker deployment — the recommended path on Linux","locale":"en"},"2366":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-73","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"One-click install (experimental) — Alpha script, including host-mode units","locale":"en"},"2367":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-74","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"Mobile (Android) — running this manual flow on Android","locale":"en"},"2368":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-75","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"Updating","locale":"en"},"2369":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual-76","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/linux-manual#see-also","content":"Configuration","locale":"en"},"2370":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"page","content":"Mobile (Android, experimental)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Deployment"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","locale":"en"},"2371":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","content":"Run SnowLuma on Android via Termux + a proot Ubuntu userland","locale":"en"},"2372":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"Reality check: Docker barely works on a phone","locale":"en"},"2373":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-1-install-termux","content":"Step 1: Install Termux","locale":"en"},"2374":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-2-install-a-proot-ubuntu","content":"Step 2: Install a proot Ubuntu","locale":"en"},"2375":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Step 3: Run the Linux-manual flow inside proot Ubuntu","locale":"en"},"2376":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"Honest limitations","locale":"en"},"2377":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#on-docker-on-a-phone","content":"On \"Docker on a phone\"","locale":"en"},"2378":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#see-also","content":"See also","locale":"en"},"2379":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","content":"Mobile deployment is **experimental and advanced**, it's resource-heavy, and it isn't for most people. If you have a PC or server, prefer Docker deployment. This page only describes the realistic \"it can run\" path on Android and is honest about its limits.","locale":"en"},"2380":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"Set expectations first:","locale":"en"},"2381":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"**A no-root Linux userland on Android is easy** — install Termux + a Ubuntu via `proot-distro`.","locale":"en"},"2382":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"**But real Docker (dockerd) does not work under proot.** proot is just a ptrace-based syscall emulator; it has no namespaces / cgroups / netfilter, which is exactly what a container runtime depends on.","locale":"en"},"2383":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"Running **real Docker** on a phone requires a VM (QEMU inside Termux, or apps like Podroid). Phones generally have no KVM, so it falls back to TCG software emulation, which is **very slow** and impractical for almost everyone. Google's AVF \"Linux Terminal\" is a real VM, but its stock image still isn't container-ready as of 2026.","locale":"en"},"2384":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"**So the realistic recommendation on mobile: Termux + proot Ubuntu, then run SnowLuma manually following the Linux (manual) flow inside it.** Docker on a phone is for tinkerers and is not recommended here.","locale":"en"},"2385":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#reality-check-docker-barely-works-on-a-phone","content":"For a per-platform comparison see Choosing a deployment.","locale":"en"},"2386":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-1-install-termux","content":"**Do not install Termux from Google Play.** The Play Store build is long-abandoned and has known issues. Install from **F-Droid** or Termux's GitHub Releases.","locale":"en"},"2387":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-1-install-termux","content":"F-Droid: https\\://f-droid.org/packages/com.termux/","locale":"en"},"2388":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-1-install-termux","content":"GitHub: https\\://github.com/termux/termux-app/releases","locale":"en"},"2389":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-1-install-termux","content":"After installing, update first:","locale":"en"},"2390":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-2-install-a-proot-ubuntu","content":"Once inside, you're in a Ubuntu userland. Docs: https\\://github.com/termux/proot-distro and https\\://wiki.termux.com.","locale":"en"},"2391":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"The rest is **identical** to Linux (manual); do it inside proot Ubuntu:","locale":"en"},"2392":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Install Node 24 LTS.","locale":"en"},"2393":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Install Linux QQ (`.deb`) plus Electron / CJK deps (note `libasound2t64` on Ubuntu 24.04).","locale":"en"},"2394":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Bring up Xvfb + fluxbox + x11vnc + noVNC to scan the QR.","locale":"en"},"2395":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Grant `cap_sys_ptrace` to node.","locale":"en"},"2396":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Freeze QQ's hot-update (black-hole `qqpatch.gtimg.cn` in `/etc/hosts`).","locale":"en"},"2397":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Download and unpack the SnowLuma `-lite` linux tarball, run `node ./index.mjs`.","locale":"en"},"2398":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Launch QQ, scan-login, open the WebUI.","locale":"en"},"2399":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Since everything runs on the phone itself, open noVNC / WebUI directly at `http://localhost:6081` / `http://localhost:5099` in the phone's browser.","locale":"en"},"2400":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#step-3-run-the-linux-manual-flow-inside-proot-ubuntu","content":"Follow the full commands in Linux (manual) — they aren't repeated here.","locale":"en"},"2401":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"**ptrace injection may be limited under proot.** SnowLuma's hook injects into the QQ process via ptrace, and proot is itself a ptrace emulation layer — whether injection succeeds when the two stack **depends heavily on your device, kernel, and proot version** and is not guaranteed. This is the single biggest unknown of this path.","locale":"en"},"2402":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"**Resource-heavy.** QQ is a Chromium-based Electron app, plus a full Xvfb + VNC + Node stack — heavy on a phone's RAM, heat, and battery. Low-end devices won't cope.","locale":"en"},"2403":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"**arm64.** Phones are almost always arm64 — pick the `arm64` builds of Node, Linux QQ, and SnowLuma.","locale":"en"},"2404":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"**Session persistence.** Termux processes can be killed by the system's battery saver; use Termux's wake-lock and consider disabling battery optimization for the app.","locale":"en"},"2405":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"**Wrong arch won't run.** Use the `linux-arm64-lite` tarball and the arm64 QQ `.deb`.","locale":"en"},"2406":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#honest-limitations","content":"Overall: &#x2A;*mobile deployment is fine for tinkering or emergencies, but it's not a stable production setup.** For long-term stability, use Docker deployment on a PC or server.","locale":"en"},"2407":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#on-docker-on-a-phone","content":"If you insist on Docker on a phone, the only viable direction is to run a QEMU Linux VM inside Termux, install real Docker in the VM, and follow Docker deployment. But software-only emulation without KVM is very slow and **not recommended** — noted here for completeness only.","locale":"en"},"2408":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#see-also","content":"Choosing a deployment","locale":"en"},"2409":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#see-also","content":"Linux (manual) — the core steps live here","locale":"en"},"2410":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-39","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#see-also","content":"Docker deployment — the genuinely recommended path","locale":"en"},"2411":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile-40","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/mobile#see-also","content":"Configuration","locale":"en"},"2412":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"page","content":"Migrate Docker persistent data","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Deployment"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","locale":"en"},"2413":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"Safely move an existing Docker deployment to the current persistent storage layout","locale":"en"},"2414":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#1-identify-the-existing-container","content":"1\\. Identify the existing container","locale":"en"},"2415":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#2-stop-the-container","content":"2\\. Stop the container","locale":"en"},"2416":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#3-create-the-current-volumes","content":"3\\. Create the current volumes","locale":"en"},"2417":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#4-copy-and-verify-the-data","content":"4\\. Copy and verify the data","locale":"en"},"2418":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#5-update-the-compose-configuration","content":"5\\. Update the Compose configuration","locale":"en"},"2419":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"6\\. Verify the migration","locale":"en"},"2420":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#bind-mounts-and-multiple-accounts","content":"Bind mounts and multiple accounts","locale":"en"},"2421":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#host-directories","content":"Host directories","locale":"en"},"2422":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#multiple-accounts","content":"Multiple accounts","locale":"en"},"2423":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"The current image uses a consistent persistent storage layout. Docker does not copy data between named volumes automatically, so an existing deployment must be migrated once when moving from the previous layout.","locale":"en"},"2424":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"This procedure preserves SnowLuma and OneBot configuration, the QQ login state, and local QQ data. It does not delete the previous volumes.","locale":"en"},"2425":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"Keep the previous container. Do not run `docker compose down` or remove it yet.","locale":"en"},"2426":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"Never run `docker compose down -v`; it removes Compose-managed volumes.","locale":"en"},"2427":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"The container must be stopped during the copy, so allow for a short interruption.","locale":"en"},"2428":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","content":"Keep enough free disk space for the old and new data to coexist during migration.","locale":"en"},"2429":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#1-identify-the-existing-container","content":"The commands below use the default container name, `snowluma`. Change the first line if you use another name.","locale":"en"},"2430":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#1-identify-the-existing-container","content":"The output must include these container paths:","locale":"en"},"2431":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#1-identify-the-existing-container","content":"Stop if any path is missing and check the existing deployment configuration.","locale":"en"},"2432":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#2-stop-the-container","content":"Stopping the container prevents configuration databases or QQ login data from changing during the copy.","locale":"en"},"2433":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#2-stop-the-container","content":"Do not remove the container. The copy command reads its existing mounts directly, including volume names generated by Compose.","locale":"en"},"2434":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#3-create-the-current-volumes","content":"Confirm that every destination volume is empty:","locale":"en"},"2435":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#3-create-the-current-volumes","content":"If this fails, do not overwrite the destination. First determine whether it belongs to an earlier incomplete migration.","locale":"en"},"2436":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#4-copy-and-verify-the-data","content":"The following command mounts the previous container data read-only, copies it into the current volumes, and verifies every regular file. It may download the small `alpine` image on first use.","locale":"en"},"2437":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#4-copy-and-verify-the-data","content":"The command must print:","locale":"en"},"2438":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#4-copy-and-verify-the-data","content":"If any step fails, stop the migration. The previous data remains in its original volumes.","locale":"en"},"2439":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#5-update-the-compose-configuration","content":"Use the current mounts and explicit volume names:","locale":"en"},"2440":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#5-update-the-compose-configuration","content":"Pull and start the current image:","locale":"en"},"2441":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#5-update-the-compose-configuration","content":"For a `docker run` deployment, remove the stopped container, recreate it with the same ports and environment, and use these mounts:","locale":"en"},"2442":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"Confirm each item before considering the migration complete:","locale":"en"},"2443":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"The container remains running without repeated startup errors.","locale":"en"},"2444":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"The WebUI accepts the existing credentials and shows the previous settings.","locale":"en"},"2445":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"QQ keeps its login state without requiring another QR login.","locale":"en"},"2446":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"OneBot listeners, tokens, and bot connections match the previous deployment.","locale":"en"},"2447":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"A test message can be received and sent successfully.","locale":"en"},"2448":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"Keep the previous Compose file and volumes until the service has remained healthy through at least one container restart. To roll back, stop the current container, restore the previous Compose file and image version, and start it again.","locale":"en"},"2449":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#6-verify-the-migration","content":"This procedure never deletes the previous volumes.","locale":"en"},"2450":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#host-directories","content":"If step 1 reports `bind` mounts, the data already lives in host directories and does not need to be copied into named volumes. Keep the host source directory and change only the application-data destination from `/app/snowluma-data` to `/app/data`. The two QQ destinations remain unchanged.","locale":"en"},"2451":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/storage-migration#multiple-accounts","content":"Additional QQ account destinations remain `/app/qq-acct2`, `/app/qq-acct3`, and so on. To rename those volumes as well, create one new volume per account, repeat the copy and verification process, and keep each account on its own complete `HOME`.","locale":"en"},"2452":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","type":"page","content":"Updating","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Deployment"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","locale":"en"},"2453":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","content":"Upgrade SnowLuma on every deployment path without losing your login or config","locale":"en"},"2454":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#docker","content":"Docker","locale":"en"},"2455":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#manual-tarball","content":"Manual tarball","locale":"en"},"2456":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#build-from-source","content":"Build from source","locale":"en"},"2457":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#version-pinning-and-the-qq-version","content":"Version pinning and the QQ version","locale":"en"},"2458":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","content":"There's one core rule for upgrading SnowLuma: **replace the program, not the data**. Your login, per-account config, and runtime config all live in the data dir / data volumes — keep them and you won't have to re-scan a QR or reconfigure.","locale":"en"},"2459":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","content":"The WebUI surfaces an update check, so you can see when a new version is available.","locale":"en"},"2460":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#docker","content":"Easiest. With Compose:","locale":"en"},"2461":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#docker","content":"Or pull the image manually and recreate the container:","locale":"en"},"2462":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#docker","content":"**Do not delete the data volumes** (`qq-gateway-data`, `qq-client-config`, `qq-client-data`). Deleting them loses your login and config. `docker rm` only removes the container, not the volumes — recreate with the same volume names and it picks up right where it left off.","locale":"en"},"2463":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#manual-tarball","content":"Download the new release tarball for your platform / arch from GitHub Releases — same flavor (full or lite) you were using.","locale":"en"},"2464":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#manual-tarball","content":"Replace the program files (`index.mjs`, `webui/`, `native/`, etc.), but **keep your config / data dir** (`config/` and its sibling data).","locale":"en"},"2465":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#manual-tarball","content":"Restart: `node ./index.mjs` (on Windows you can also double-click `launcher.bat`).","locale":"en"},"2466":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#manual-tarball","content":"Keep your data dir outside the unpacked folder, or back it up before upgrading, so you can replace the whole directory without accidentally wiping config.","locale":"en"},"2467":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#build-from-source","content":"`dist/` is regenerated (`index.mjs`, `launcher.bat`, `webui/`, `native/`). Again, **leave your data dir untouched** and just restart.","locale":"en"},"2468":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#version-pinning-and-the-qq-version","content":"The bundled QQ version is **pinned** to the native hook. Upgrading SnowLuma itself is safe, but **do not manually update the QQ** inside the container / environment — once QQ's version changes, the version-pinned native hook may break.","locale":"en"},"2469":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#version-pinning-and-the-qq-version","content":"The Docker image already freezes the QQ version by black-holing QQ's silent hot-update host (see Linux manual deployment). On manual / non-Docker setups you should likewise prevent QQ from auto-updating.","locale":"en"},"2470":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/update#version-pinning-and-the-qq-version","content":"In production, pin a specific image / release tag (e.g. `motricseven7/snowluma:v1.10.0`) instead of riding `:latest`, so upgrades are explicit, controlled, and rollback-able.","locale":"en"},"2471":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","type":"page","content":"Windows · Docker Desktop","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Deployment"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","locale":"en"},"2472":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","content":"Run SnowLuma's Linux image on Windows via Docker Desktop's WSL2 backend","locale":"en"},"2473":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#install-docker-desktop","content":"Install Docker Desktop","locale":"en"},"2474":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"Requirements","locale":"en"},"2475":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#run-the-snowluma-container","content":"Run the SnowLuma container","locale":"en"},"2476":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"Access","locale":"en"},"2477":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#where-to-go-next","content":"Where to go next","locale":"en"},"2478":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","content":"**Up front: the SnowLuma Docker image is Linux.** It runs **headless Linux QQ** + Xvfb + VNC + noVNC inside the container. On Windows you run this Linux container through Docker Desktop's **WSL2 backend** — i.e. it's still running inside a lightweight Linux VM.","locale":"en"},"2479":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","content":"If you want to run **native Windows QQ** (`QQ.exe`) on Windows, don't use Docker — see Windows (manual) instead. This page is for \"I just want a container and don't want to touch native QQ.\"","locale":"en"},"2480":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","content":"Once running, the container is **exactly the same** as in Docker (Linux) deployment — same image, same `docker run` / Compose, same ports. This page only covers the Windows-side prerequisites.","locale":"en"},"2481":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#install-docker-desktop","content":"Download `Docker Desktop Installer.exe` from docs.docker.com and install it with the **default WSL2 backend**.","locale":"en"},"2482":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"Windows 10 22H2 or Windows 11 23H2 and newer","locale":"en"},"2483":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"WSL ≥ 2.1.5","locale":"en"},"2484":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"8 GB RAM","locale":"en"},"2485":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"Virtualization enabled in BIOS (VT-x / AMD-V), plus SLAT","locale":"en"},"2486":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"When you keep the WSL2 backend selected during install (the default), Docker Desktop installs / enables WSL2 for you. Reboot afterward and start Docker Desktop.","locale":"en"},"2487":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"Confirm you're on the WSL2 backend and not the old Hyper-V backend: Docker Desktop → Settings → General → check **Use the WSL 2 based engine**.","locale":"en"},"2488":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#requirements","content":"If you'd rather install / manage WSL2 yourself (or run native Docker Engine inside a WSL distro instead of Docker Desktop), see WSL2.","locale":"en"},"2489":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#run-the-snowluma-container","content":"With Docker Desktop ready, use the same command from the canonical Docker page. Open PowerShell:","locale":"en"},"2490":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#run-the-snowluma-container","content":"`--cap-add=SYS_PTRACE` and `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` are **still required**. SnowLuma's native addon injects the hook into the container's QQ process via ptrace, which the default seccomp profile blocks. Docker Desktop's WSL2 backend passes these through to the WSL2 kernel, so they work as usual.","locale":"en"},"2491":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#run-the-snowluma-container","content":"The full reference — Compose, environment variables, volumes, multi-account — is **not repeated here**; everything is identical to Linux, so see Docker (Linux) deployment. Compose works the same from PowerShell:","locale":"en"},"2492":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"Reach the container's services directly via `localhost` (Docker Desktop forwards the ports to the Windows host):","locale":"en"},"2493":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"URL","locale":"en"},"2494":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"2495":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"`http://localhost:6081/`","locale":"en"},"2496":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"noVNC — view the remote desktop, scan the QR for QQ","locale":"en"},"2497":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"`http://localhost:5099/`","locale":"en"},"2498":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"SnowLuma WebUI","locale":"en"},"2499":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"`http://localhost:3000/`","locale":"en"},"2500":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"OneBot HTTP","locale":"en"},"2501":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"`ws://localhost:3001/`","locale":"en"},"2502":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"OneBot WebSocket","locale":"en"},"2503":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"The first-login flow (noVNC QR scan, finding the WebUI temporary password, etc.) is identical to Linux — see Docker (Linux) deployment · first login.","locale":"en"},"2504":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#access","content":"There is **no** shared default remote-desktop password: the first start generates a random one and prints it once in the container logs. Do not expose noVNC / WebUI / OneBot ports raw on the public internet. Full notes: Docker deployment · Remote-desktop password.","locale":"en"},"2505":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#where-to-go-next","content":"Docker (Linux) deployment — the **full reference** for the image, ports, Compose, env vars, volumes, and multi-account. This page is just the Windows prerequisites.","locale":"en"},"2506":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#where-to-go-next","content":"WSL2 — prefer not to use Docker Desktop? Install native Docker Engine inside a WSL distro and run the same container.","locale":"en"},"2507":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-docker#where-to-go-next","content":"Windows (manual) — to run native Windows QQ (`QQ.exe`) instead of the container's Linux QQ.","locale":"en"},"2508":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"page","content":"Windows (manual)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Deployment"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","locale":"en"},"2509":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","content":"Run SnowLuma natively on Windows — the hook auto-injects the desktop QQ client (QQ.exe)","locale":"en"},"2510":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites","locale":"en"},"2511":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#1-install-the-desktop-qq-client-ntqq","content":"1\\. Install the desktop QQ client (NTQQ)","locale":"en"},"2512":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"2\\. Download a SnowLuma release","locale":"en"},"2513":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#3-lite-only-install-nodejs","content":"3\\. (Lite only) Install Node.js","locale":"en"},"2514":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#unpack-and-run","content":"Unpack and run","locale":"en"},"2515":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#launch-order-of-qq-and-snowluma","content":"Launch order of QQ and SnowLuma","locale":"en"},"2516":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#login","content":"Login","locale":"en"},"2517":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#disable-auto-injection-optional","content":"Disable auto-injection (optional)","locale":"en"},"2518":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"Accessing the WebUI and OneBot","locale":"en"},"2519":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#finding-the-webui-temporary-password","content":"Finding the WebUI temporary password","locale":"en"},"2520":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"Where config lands","locale":"en"},"2521":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#firewall","content":"Firewall","locale":"en"},"2522":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#next-steps","content":"Next steps","locale":"en"},"2523":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","content":"On Windows, SnowLuma runs **natively** — no Docker, no WSL2 needed. Install the desktop QQ client, download and unpack the matching SnowLuma release, and run it. SnowLuma discovers `QQ.exe` and injects the hook automatically.","locale":"en"},"2524":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","content":"> Want to run it in a container instead? See Windows · Docker Desktop (note: the image is Linux and runs Linux QQ).","locale":"en"},"2525":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#1-install-the-desktop-qq-client-ntqq","content":"Download and install the Windows desktop QQ client (the NT architecture) from Tencent's official site. Make sure `QQ.exe` launches normally. SnowLuma injects into this running NTQQ process.","locale":"en"},"2526":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#1-install-the-desktop-qq-client-ntqq","content":"Version matching matters. SnowLuma's native hook is aligned to a specific QQ version. If QQ auto-updates to a newer version SnowLuma doesn't support yet, the hook may fail to load — check the WebUI / README version-compatibility notes if you hit trouble.","locale":"en"},"2527":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"Go to GitHub Releases, pick `win-x64`, and choose one of two flavors:","locale":"en"},"2528":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"Package","locale":"en"},"2529":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"Notes","locale":"en"},"2530":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"`SnowLuma-<TAG>-win-x64.tar.gz`","locale":"en"},"2531":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"**Full** — bundles a Node.js runtime, no separate Node install, larger.","locale":"en"},"2532":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"`SnowLuma-<TAG>-win-x64-lite.tar.gz`","locale":"en"},"2533":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"**Lite** — no Node.js, you install Node yourself first, much smaller.","locale":"en"},"2534":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"Replace `<TAG>` with the version you want (e.g. `v1.10.0`).","locale":"en"},"2535":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#2-download-a-snowluma-release","content":"If you don't want to fuss, grab the **full** package — it ships Node and runs straight out of the box.","locale":"en"},"2536":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#3-lite-only-install-nodejs","content":"SnowLuma needs **Node.js ≥ 22**. As of 2026, **Node 24 (\"Krypton\") is Active LTS** and 22 (\"Jod\") is Maintenance LTS. For the lite package, install **Node 24 LTS** (22 still works).","locale":"en"},"2537":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#3-lite-only-install-nodejs","content":"Download the Windows installer from nodejs.org, then verify in PowerShell:","locale":"en"},"2538":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#3-lite-only-install-nodejs","content":"The full package ships its own runtime — **skip this step**.","locale":"en"},"2539":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#unpack-and-run","content":"Unpack the tarball into a fixed directory, e.g. `C:\\SnowLuma\\`. After unpacking you'll find `index.mjs`, `launcher.bat`, `webui/`, `native/`, etc.","locale":"en"},"2540":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#unpack-and-run","content":"`.tar.gz` can be unpacked with the built-in `tar` (shipped on Win10/11):","locale":"en"},"2541":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#unpack-and-run","content":"7-Zip and similar tools work too.","locale":"en"},"2542":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#unpack-and-run","content":"From the unpacked directory, double-click `launcher.bat` (it just runs `node ./index.mjs` then `pause` so you can read the output). Or run it manually from a terminal:","locale":"en"},"2543":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#unpack-and-run","content":"If the full package uses its bundled Node, follow the README's launch instructions (usually `launcher.bat` is enough); see WebUI / README for specifics.","locale":"en"},"2544":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#launch-order-of-qq-and-snowluma","content":"SnowLuma has **hook auto-load** on by default: it continuously probes the system for QQ main processes, and when it finds `QQ.exe` it injects the hook automatically (starting in passive observation). After you finish the QR login, the hook switches from passive observation to working mode.","locale":"en"},"2545":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#launch-order-of-qq-and-snowluma","content":"So either order works:","locale":"en"},"2546":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#launch-order-of-qq-and-snowluma","content":"**QQ first, then SnowLuma**: SnowLuma finds the already-running `QQ.exe` and injects on startup.","locale":"en"},"2547":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#launch-order-of-qq-and-snowluma","content":"**SnowLuma first, then QQ**: SnowLuma waits until `QQ.exe` appears, then injects.","locale":"en"},"2548":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-39","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#launch-order-of-qq-and-snowluma","content":"**Run SnowLuma as the same Windows user as QQ.** Injection needs access to the target process; if SnowLuma and QQ run under different users (or mismatched privileges), injection fails. Running one elevated (as administrator) and the other not can also block injection — keep their privileges consistent.","locale":"en"},"2549":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-40","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#login","content":"Login is **QR-scan only**: scan the QR in the QQ window on your desktop with the QQ mobile app. On native Windows you can see the QQ window directly — no VNC/noVNC needed (that's only for Linux headless setups).","locale":"en"},"2550":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-41","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#disable-auto-injection-optional","content":"To keep the traditional \"Load from the WebUI\" flow, set `hookAutoLoad` to `false` in `runtime.json`, or set the env var `SNOWLUMA_HOOK_AUTOLOAD=0`. Env vars take precedence.","locale":"en"},"2551":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-42","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"Once running, SnowLuma listens on these ports by default:","locale":"en"},"2552":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-43","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"Port","locale":"en"},"2553":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-44","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"2554":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-45","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"`5099`","locale":"en"},"2555":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-46","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"SnowLuma WebUI","locale":"en"},"2556":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-47","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"`3000`","locale":"en"},"2557":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-48","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"OneBot HTTP (default)","locale":"en"},"2558":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-49","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"`3001`","locale":"en"},"2559":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-50","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"OneBot WebSocket (default)","locale":"en"},"2560":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-51","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#accessing-the-webui-and-onebot","content":"Open the WebUI in a browser:","locale":"en"},"2561":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-52","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#finding-the-webui-temporary-password","content":"On the **first start with a fresh data dir**, a one-time admin password is printed to the console / logs. Scroll through the console window that `launcher.bat` opens (look for the line containing \"临时密码 / initial credentials\").","locale":"en"},"2562":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-53","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#finding-the-webui-temporary-password","content":"The temporary password is printed **only on the first start with a fresh data dir** — reusing an existing dir or restarting won't regenerate it. See the WebUI / README for the exact wording.","locale":"en"},"2563":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-54","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"The first run auto-creates config files under the data dir:","locale":"en"},"2564":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-55","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"File","locale":"en"},"2565":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-56","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"2566":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-57","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"`config/onebot.json`","locale":"en"},"2567":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-58","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"Global default OneBot config.","locale":"en"},"2568":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-59","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"`config/onebot_<uin>.json`","locale":"en"},"2569":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-60","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"One per account (by UIN).","locale":"en"},"2570":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-61","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"`config/runtime.json`","locale":"en"},"2571":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-62","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"Runtime settings: WebUI port, `hookAutoLoad`, log level…","locale":"en"},"2572":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-63","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"Env vars take precedence over `runtime.json`. Multi-account needs no manual config: each UIN you log in spins up its own OneBot instance. Full details in the Configuration reference.","locale":"en"},"2573":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-64","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#where-config-lands","content":"The exact data-dir location is in the WebUI / README (it can differ by packaging).","locale":"en"},"2574":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-65","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#firewall","content":"On first start, the Windows Firewall may prompt whether to allow `node` / SnowLuma network access.","locale":"en"},"2575":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-66","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#firewall","content":"For **local-only** access (`127.0.0.1`), you don't need to allow public access.","locale":"en"},"2576":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-67","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#firewall","content":"To let other devices on your LAN reach the WebUI / OneBot, allow the corresponding ports (`5099` / `3000` / `3001`) through the firewall.","locale":"en"},"2577":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-68","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#firewall","content":"**Never expose the WebUI / OneBot ports raw on the public internet.** These ports give full control over your QQ. For remote access, go through a reverse proxy with auth or an authenticated tunnel, and change the default credentials.","locale":"en"},"2578":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-69","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows#next-steps","content":"Configuration reference — OneBot / runtime options explained.","locale":"en"},"2579":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows-70","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/deploy/wi